from biology_course_maps import p,m
MAPS={3:[
m('00-inspector-calls-overview','An Inspector Calls overview',[],[
p('Act 1: power exposed','Setting: Brumley, 1912. Engagement celebration is interrupted by Goole.','Arthur: factory dismissal after a pay strike. Sheila: shop dismissal caused by jealousy.','Meaning: comfortable lives can depend on unequal power.'),
p('Act 2: appearances break','Gerald: protection and shelter coexist with an unequal affair. Sheila returns the ring.','Sybil: class prejudice blocks charitable help. She condemns the father before identifying Eric.','Method: disclosure and dramatic irony increase pressure.'),
p('Act 3: lesson tested','Eric: coercive conduct, pregnancy and stolen money. Eva refuses stolen funds.','Inspector: connects harm and social duties. Identity later becomes doubtful.','Sheila and Eric retain guilt. Others welcome relief. Final call renews investigation.'),
p('Characters and choices','Arthur and Sybil: status, confidence and resistance. Sheila and Eric: faults, remorse and learning.','Gerald: helpful actions and unequal control. Goole: moral investigation. Eva: absent, but morally active.','Judge each motive. Do not assume all harms are identical.'),
p('Shared responsibility','Class: employers, customers and charities control Eva’s options. Gender: economic and sexual vulnerability.','Generations: changed attitudes contrast with restored complacency. Reputation: respectability conceals harm.','Central question: is avoiding punishment the same as acting responsibly?'),
p('The ending reopens the case','Cycle: apparent escape is overturned. The lesson still concerns admitted conduct.','Audience: Goole’s identity remains uncertain. The ambiguity does not erase responsibility.')]),
m('01-characters-and-responsibility','Characters and responsibility',[],[
p('Arthur Birling','Role: manufacturer seeking business and status. Dismisses Eva after a strike.','Language: commercial priorities frame people through cost and profit.','Irony: wrong predictions weaken authority. Ending: welcomes possible escape.'),
p('Sybil Birling','Role: socially confident charity leader. Rejects Eva through prejudice.','Language: distancing class labels replace attention to individual need.','Irony: condemns the father before learning he is Eric. Status does not guarantee care.'),
p('Sheila Birling','Start: jealousy plus customer influence costs Eva a job.','Development: admits harm, questions Gerald and challenges comfortable appearances.','Ending: responsibility survives doubts about Goole. Growth does not erase guilt.'),
p('Eric Birling','Conduct: drinking, coercion and stolen money create serious harm.','Language: evasive descriptions reveal discomfort. Financial help does not excuse earlier conduct.','Ending: admits responsibility. He is flawed, not a simple moral hero.'),
p('Gerald Croft','Position: socially desirable fiancé. Helps Daisy but holds more power in the affair.','Development: confesses, then investigates Goole and shares the family’s relief.','Interpretation: recognise kindness and self-interest together.'),
p('Goole and Eva','Goole: directs questions, challenges evasions and broadens the moral message. Identity stays unresolved.','Eva: never appears. Others control the telling of her life.','Agency: seeks fair pay and refuses stolen money. Represents vulnerable ordinary people.')]),
m('02-themes-and-stagecraft','Themes and stagecraft',[],[
p('Responsibility and guilt','Pattern: separate choices combine to narrow Eva’s options.','Contrast: genuine acceptance versus fear of scandal. Remorse matters if values change.','Ending: technical doubts do not undo admitted conduct.'),
p('Class, money and gender','Power: Arthur controls work. Sheila influences a shop. Sybil controls aid.','Gerald and Eric: greater social and economic power affects relationships.','Contrast: secure dining room versus Eva’s insecurity outside it.'),
p('Generations and appearances','Sheila and Eric become more willing to learn. Their original faults remain significant.','Gerald complicates a simple age divide. Sybil also shows women can defend hierarchy.','Respectability: public roles conceal harmful behaviour.'),
p('Irony and revelation','1912 setting: audience knows Arthur’s confident predictions are wrong.','Sybil: condemns the unknown father before identifying her own son.','Ordered disclosures: suspense turns a mystery into a moral enquiry.'),
p('What the stage shows','Lighting: comfortable atmosphere becomes exposed. Single setting: pressure accumulates.','Doorbell: interrupts self-interest. Act endings: recognition and anticipated confession create suspense.','Goole: detective and morality-play features, without a proven supernatural identity.'),
p('A developed answer','Task: use the printed extract and the wider play. Track the character or idea across moments.','Evidence: short quotation or precise event. Method: explain meaning and stage effect.','Judgement: connect development to the question. Avoid retelling or unrelated history.')])
]}
